What happened during import?

the TSV remained a data.frame;
the BED coordinates were converted into a GRanges object;
the GFF3 gene features became another GRanges object;
FaFile points to the indexed FASTA without loading the whole genome as an ordinary character string;
scanFaIndex() returns the sequence ranges available in the FASTA index.


5.1 Add stable peak and gene identifiers
BED column 4 is imported as the name metadata column. Add:
if (!"name" %in% names(mcols(peaks))) {
  stop("Consensus BED must contain peak IDs in column 4")
}
peaks$peak_id &lt;- as.character(peaks$name)
names(peaks) &lt;- peaks$peak_id
GFF3 attribute names vary. Select the first suitable gene-identifier field:
candidate_id_columns &lt;- c("ID", "gene_id", "locus_tag", "Name")
gene_id_column &lt;- intersect(candidate_id_columns, names(mcols(genes)))[1]

if (is.na(gene_id_column)) {
  stop("No suitable gene identifier found in the GFF3 gene features")
}

genes$gene_id &lt;- as.character(mcols(genes)[[gene_id_column]])
names(genes) &lt;- genes$gene_id
Set chromosome lengths from the FASTA index so that interval trimming is explicit:
genome_lengths &lt;- setNames(width(genome_index), as.character(seqnames(genome_index)))
common_seqlevels &lt;- intersect(names(genome_lengths), union(seqlevels(peaks), seqlevels(genes)))

seqlengths(peaks)[common_seqlevels] &lt;- genome_lengths[common_seqlevels]
seqlengths(genes)[common_seqlevels] &lt;- genome_lengths[common_seqlevels]
Check that peaks and genes share sequence names:
shared_sequences &lt;- intersect(seqlevelsInUse(peaks), seqlevelsInUse(genes))
if (length(shared_sequences) == 0) {
  stop("Peaks and genes do not share chromosome names")
}
shared_sequences

13. What to keep from this optional day
The transferable ideas are more important than the exact thresholds:

genomic interval operations should be done with interval-aware objects;
nearest-gene and promoter-overlap annotations answer different questions;
strand changes the location of an upstream promoter window;
a Hits object stores relationships between interval sets;
an indexed FASTA can return only the requested sequences;
DNA sequence composition is a property of the intervals, not evidence of regulation;
ChIP-seq binding plus RNA-seq change generates a hypothesis, not a proof.

What you will have ready today
By the end of Day 0, you should be able to:

access GitHub;
access Docker Hub;
install and open Visual Studio Code;
install Git and verify that it works;
install Docker and verify that it can run a container;
install and open IGV.

These steps should be completed before the start of the workshop.
For any problems, questions, or installation issues, follow the issue-reporting instructions at the end of this page.

6. Report a setup problem
If a setup problem remains after checking the table above, email:
francesco.gualdrini@gmail.com
Use this exact email subject:
Issue - Reproducible NGS workshop
In the message, include:

your operating system and version;
the Day 0 step where the problem occurred;
the command you ran;
the complete error message or a screenshot of it;
which readiness checks completed successfully.

Do not include passwords, access tokens, or account recovery codes.

13. Checkpoint route
If mapping or peak calling is too slow on a laptop, continue from checkpoint files generated against GCF_025998455.1 and NZ_AP026446.1.
At minimum, record:

which upstream steps were demonstrated but not executed locally;
which checkpoint files were used;
the reference assembly and annotation versions;
which downstream steps you executed yourself.
